#Problem APEX 5.1.26

## DBsubject(Calculus - single variable)
## DBchapter(Integrals)
## DBsection(Indefinite integrals (without trigonometric functions))
## Institution(Valdosta State University)
## KEYWORDS(indefinite integral)
## Author(S. V. Ault)
## Level(3)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('APEX Calculus')
## AuthorText1('Hartman')
## EditionText1('3.0')
## Section1('5.1')
## Problem1('26')

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context('Numeric')->variables->add(a => 'Real', t => 'Real');
Context()->flags->set(reduceConstants => 0);

$integranda = "x";
$integrandb = "a";
$integrandc = "t^2";

### Answers
$antideriva = Formula("x^2/2");
$antiderivb = Formula("a x");
$antiderivc = Formula("t^2 x");

BEGIN_PGML
Evaluate the given indefinite integrals.

a) [``\int [$integranda]  \, dx ``] = [____]{$antideriva->cmp(upToConstant=>1)}{30} [` + C`].

b) [``\int [$integrandb]  \, dx ``] = [____]{$antiderivb->cmp(upToConstant=>1)}{30} [` + C`].

c) [``\int [$integrandc]  \, dx ``] = [____]{$antiderivc->cmp(upToConstant=>1)}{30} [` + C`].
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_HINT
Be careful: The differential indicates which variable is to be considered independent.  Treat all other variables as constant: [` \int [$integrandb]  \, dx = a \int \, dx `] and [` \int [$integrandc]  \, dx  =  t^2 \int   \, dx`].
END_PGML_HINT

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION

Be careful: The differential indicates which variable is to be considered independent.  Treat all other variables as constant.

a) [``\int x \, dx = \dfrac{x^2}{2} + C.``]

b) [`` \int a \, dx = ax + C.``]

c) [``\int t^2 \, dx = t^2 x + C.``]

END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();

